/P%27an_Shih-%C3%AAn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/P&#39;an Shih-\u00ean\">P'an Shih-\u00ean</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan_Lei\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Pan Lei\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367633\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367633\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3649315</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 2 \u2014 P&#39;an Lei</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Fang Chao-ying</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"606\" data-page-number=\"606\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/4\" data-page-index=\"4\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/4\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;4\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>P'AN</b> Lei \u6f58\u8012 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u6b21\u8015,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u7a3c\u5802, \u6b62\u6b62\u5c45\u58eb), 1646\u20131708, Nov. 11, scholar, was a native of Wu-chiang, Kiangsu, and a younger half-brother of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/P%27an_Ch%27%C3%AAng-chang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/P&#39;an Ch&#39;\u00eang-chang\">P'an Ch'\u00eang-chang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. When the latter was involved in the case of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chuang_T%27ing-lung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chuang T&#39;ing-lung\">Chuang T'ing-lung</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] P'an Lei lived in disguise in his mother's home under the assumed name of Wu Ch'i \u5433\u7426. He studied under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ku_Yen-wu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ku Yen-wu\">Ku Yen-wu</a></span>, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Hs%C3%BC_Fang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Hs\u00fc Fang\">Hs\u00fc Fang</a></span>&#32;[<i>qq.&#8239;v.</i>] and Tai Li \u6234\u7b20 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u8018\u91ce,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u66fc\u516c,&#32;original <i>ming</i> \u9f0e\u7acb, T. \u5247\u4e4b), and in 1679 was summoned to Peking to take the special examination known as <i>po-hs\u00fceh hung-tz'\u016d</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/P%27%C3%AAng_Sun-y%C3%BC\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/P&#39;\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc\">P'\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc</a></span>) which he passed as the youngest scholar on the list. Among the fifty successful competitors only he, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chu_I-tsun\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chu I-tsun\">Chu I-tsun</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], Li Yin-tu (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ch%27%C3%BC_Ta-ch%C3%BCn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ch&#39;\u00fc Ta-ch\u00fcn\">Ch'\u00fc Ta-ch\u00fcn</a></span>), and Yen Sh\u00eang-sun \u56b4\u7e69\u5b6b (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u84c0\u53cb,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u85d5\u6f01,&#32;1623\u20131702) had not previously held an official post nor acquired a degree\u2014a coincidence that caused them to be known as \"the four cotton-clothed scholars\" \u56db\u5e03\u8863, or commoners.\n</p><p>Made a Hanlin corrector and appointed one of the compilers of the history of the Ming Dynasty (<i>Ming-shih</i>), he edited the section on political economy, known as <i>shih-huo chih</i> \u98df\u8ca8\u5fd7, and the biographical sketches (<i>lieh-</i><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"607\" data-page-number=\"607\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/5\" data-page-index=\"5\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/5\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;5\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><i>chuan</i> \u5217\u50b3) that deal with the early years of that dynasty. Young, scholarly, and unacquainted with the ways of officialdom, he was degraded in 1684 on the charge of being \"impatient and petulant\". He at once resigned, and although his official title was restored in 1703, he devoted the remaining years to travel and literary pursuits. His collected essays and poems, entitled \u9042\u521d\u5802\u96c6 <i>Sui-ch'u t'ang chi</i>, in 40 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>, printed in 1710; and a work on phonology, entitled \u985e\u97f3 <i>Lei-yin</i>, in 8 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>, printed in 1712, received notice in the <i>Imperial Catalogue</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chi_Y%C3%BCn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chi Y\u00fcn\">Chi Y\u00fcn</a></span>). In 1695 P'an Lei printed the final redaction of Ku Yen-wu's well-known miscellaneous notes entitled <i>Jih-chih lu</i>. About the same time he also printed a collection of ten monographs by Ku, known as <i>T'ing-lin shih-chung</i> (for both works see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ku_Yen-wu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ku Yen-wu\">Ku Yen-wu</a></span>).\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/489/17a; 2/71/9a; 3/118/16a; 4/45/8b; 1:132/14b; 20/1/00 with portrait; 30/2/1b; 32/3/1b; <i>Ss\u016d-k'u</i> 44/96 and 183/7b; Lo Ch\u00ean-y\u00fc \u7f85\u632f\u7389, \u96f2\u7a97\u6f2b\u7a3f <i>Y\u00fcn-ch'uang man-kao</i> (1920) \u4e01/51a; Liu W\u00ean-chin \u5289\u6587\u9326, <i>Lei-yin pa</i> (\u8dcb), in <i>Bulletin of the Institute of History and Philology</i> (<i>Academia Sinica</i>), vol. I, part 4 (1930).]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Fang Chao-ying</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Ch'\u00fc Ta-ch\u00fcn"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chi Y\u00fcn"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chu I-tsun"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chuang T'ing-lung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Hs\u00fc Fang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Ku Yen-wu"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/P'an Ch'\u00eang-chang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/P'an Shih-\u00ean"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/P'\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Fang Chao-ying"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysection = p'an lei\n | previous = P'an Ch'\u00eang-chang\n | next     = P'an Shih-\u00ean\n | from     = 4\n | to       = 5\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Fang Chao-ying\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:P'an Lei }}"}}}